WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
C sharp types can express intent, not just storage shape.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:14.440
A string can hold almost anything, but a domain type can say that a value is a customer identifier, a role name, a workflow state, or a validated file label.

3
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.860
The more meaning the type carries, the less every caller has to guess.

4
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:24.660
Nullable reference types are a practical defensive tool because they make missing-value assumptions visible.

5
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:34.920
They do not magically eliminate null bugs, but they force the code to show where null is expected, where it is not expected, and where a caller must handle absence.

6
00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.340
Treat nullable warnings as design feedback, not background noise.

7
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.060
Objects should avoid incomplete or contradictory states.

8
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:54.280
If a class requires a name, identifier, date range, or configuration value, construct it through a path that validates those requirements before the object enters normal use.

9
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:01.180
Constructors, factory methods, and validation helpers can protect invariants when they are used consistently.

10
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.380
Immutability is useful when it reduces accidental state changes.

11
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:12.600
Readonly properties, records, private setters, and immutable collections can help preserve intent after construction.

12
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:19.500
Not everything needs to be immutable, but mutable state should be chosen because the design needs it, not because it was the easiest default.

13
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:28.900
Enums, value objects, records, and clear method signatures can communicate constraints better than loose strings, dictionaries, or object bags.

14
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.520
Important domain decisions should not depend on magic strings scattered across the codebase.

15
00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:41.900
When values represent real states, modes, permissions, or categories, make that meaning visible.

16
00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.860
Safer object design improves security review.

17
00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:59.000
A reviewer can reason about the system faster when invalid states are hard to create, nullability is explicit, constructors protect invariants, and method signatures show what the operation truly needs.
